WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.668
He calls himself a doctor.

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:02.268
Deep sea.

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:03.503
That's what we're going to call him, too.

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.438
Diving explorer and medical researcher

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:05.972
Joseph De

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.073
Terry just broke

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:07.540
the world

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.708
record for the longest time,

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:10.944
living underwater at ambient pressure.

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:12.846
Today is a 76th of living

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:13.413
at the Jules

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.315
Undersea Lodge habitat in Key

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:16.416
Largo, Florida.

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:17.550
And part of his research

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:17.951
is to see

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:19.719
what living at 22 feet

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:21.855
underwater does to the human body.

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:23.456
Joseph Detroit is joining us

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.692
live now from his underwater lab.

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:27.627
How are you feeling, Doctor?

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:27.894
I mean,

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:28.762
what do you think

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:31.331
this does to the human body?

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:32.866
I am so good.

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:33.900
It's coming on.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.435
I'm very honored to be on your show.

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:36.503
Thank you so much.

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:37.737
So what do I think it does

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:38.671
to the human body?

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.907
We had a null hypothesis that

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:42.542
that we would get better

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:43.710
sleep and

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.245
increase or decrease

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:47.347
our inflammatory markers in our body.

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:49.049
And the first round of blood tests

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:50.283
came back and said,

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:51.584
yeah, that's absolutely true.

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:54.120
We decreased every inflammatory marker in

00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:54.988
my body,

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:57.057
and my sleep has been so much better.

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:59.592
About 60% deep in REM sleep.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.795
So we're really excited about that.

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:03.029
I mean, that's significant.

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.731
What are the factors that you think

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:06.299
are contributing to that?

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:07.133
The things

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.769
maybe that you don't have underwater

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:11.905
or that you don't have underwater

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:12.772
that we experience

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:14.707
and maybe stress us out a little bit

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:16.676
at surface at surface

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:18.912
right?

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:19.879
Well, it's the second,

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.181
third order consequences

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:21.848
that come from

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:23.116
being in a hyper

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:24.451
meaning more than apparent

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:25.852
meaning pressure environment.

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:27.720
So I'm in a hyperbaric environment

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.555
right now,

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:29.923
and this is the exact same

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:30.723
depth and pressure

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:31.624
at which I treat

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.660
traumatic brain injury and PTSD.

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:36.362
So we knew that the inflammatory markers

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:36.963
were going to go down

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.631
because that's a mechanism of action

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:39.999
of hyperbaric medicine.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:42.535
But I'm actually at the pressure of £25

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:43.503
per square inch,

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:44.871
which is exactly where I treat

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.206
TBI and PTSD.

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:48.374
So we're watching

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:50.009
some of your daily routine,

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.678
but take us through what it is

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:52.879
like throughout the day

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:55.682
to live underwater as you do

00:01:56.649 --> 00:01:57.217
Easy.

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:58.885
So I usually wake up at between four

00:01:58.885 --> 00:01:59.686
and 5 a.m.

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.020
only because I'm an early riser.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:01.888
Always have been.

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:03.556
I'm in New York or I just got up early.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.425
It's my mom's fault. Blame her.

00:02:05.425 --> 00:02:06.025
I get up.

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:07.393
I get a couple of cups of coffee

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:08.161
because I can tell you

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:09.395
that science does not happen

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:10.463
without coffee.

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:11.898
And then I roll through my list

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:14.734
and we're doing EKGs, EEGs,

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:15.935
blood sample

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:16.903
urine, saliva,

00:02:16.903 --> 00:02:18.972
all that stuff on a daily basis.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:19.739
And, you know,

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:21.508
I'm trying to find out what happens

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:22.242
in the isolated,

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.877
confined, extreme environment,

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:26.079
which is very much akin to space travel.

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.114
So we're going to be stuck in that tube

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.216
for 200 days to get to Mars.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.385
This is just a preliminary experiment

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:33.153
to figure out

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.287
what happens when you're stuck

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:36.189
in a tube underwater for 100 days.

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:37.624
So half the time

00:02:37.624 --> 00:02:39.425
we need a couple more times on this.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:41.628
So what what other reasons?

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:41.861
I mean,

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:42.529
if this could be

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:43.830
sort of a bit of a test case

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.431
for what it is to travel to Mars

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:47.000
what are the other reasons

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.334
that you need

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.036
this research in this experience?

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:53.573
Oh, the one thing that was a

00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:54.908
totally intended

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.544
consequence is the STEM outreach to kids.

00:02:57.544 --> 00:02:59.546
We are so excited about that.

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:00.480
We've reached over

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.916
2500 kids, person-to-person

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:04.517
over Zoom calls

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.286
with people in landlocked states,

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:07.120
kids out there

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.821
that are thinking about careers

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:10.023
in science, technology,

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:11.291
engineering and math.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:12.392
And we're talking to them

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:13.226
about preserving,

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:14.527
protecting and rejuvenating

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.895
the marine environment.

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:18.231
60 to 70% of the oxygen

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:19.766
comes from the world's oceans.

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:21.334
And we have to have those kids start

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:22.735
taking better care than we did,

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:24.270
unfortunately, better care

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.505
than we did of the oceans,

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:28.341
so that we can keep giving back that life

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:29.542
sustaining oxygen

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:31.077
that we absolutely need.

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:32.612
And plus, science is cool.

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:33.513
I mean, you know,

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.014
I'm not maybe I'm not quite so cool,

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.549
but science can be very cool.

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:38.551
And you can do it in a fun environment,

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.153
like in the water.

00:03:40.153 --> 00:03:41.454
Yeah. Science is cool.

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.356
60% REM sleep is cool, too,

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:44.290
I'll tell you that. Dr.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.058
Nutri,

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:45.892
it is wonderful

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.427
to have you from your undersea

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:49.229
lab there in Key Largo, Florida.

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:50.363
Thank you for joining us.

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:52.865
Absolutely.

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:54.567
A pleasure. Thank you for having me.

